  <!-- 04 Result -->
  <section id="result" class="chapter" aria-labelledby="result-title">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="chapter-line"><span class="chapter-n" aria-hidden="true">04</span><p class="chapter-name" aria-hidden="true">Looking back</p><span class="chapter-rule" aria-hidden="true"></span></div>
      <h2 id="result-title" class="claim"><span class="visually-hidden">Looking back: </span>It shipped. Now people want more.</h2>
      <div class="prose-narrow">
        <p>JumpCloud Admin shipped on the App Store and Google Play, dark mode included, and it’s still live today. It was the company’s first native app, and it gave admins something they’d been asking for: a way to help people without being at their desks. I don’t have adoption numbers to share.</p>
        <p>The biggest miss is how much it can do. Most of the complaints in the app store reviews are about missing features. That was the trade-off of a tight MVP, and it was a real struggle internally. We chose to ship a few urgent tasks done well, knowing the app would be received that way at first.</p>
      </div>
      <h3 class="sub">Small on purpose. Maybe a little too small.</h3>
      <div class="prose-narrow">
        <p>I wish we’d been able to fit in more of the complex work, like device management. The designs were there.</p>
        <p>But I’m proud we got JumpCloud’s first native app out the door. It filled a gap the company had been missing for a long time, and it gave the team a foundation to keep adding to.</p>
      </div>
      <a class="pill live-link" href="https://apps.apple.com/app/id1596947372" rel="noopener" target="_blank">See it on the App Store <Icon name="arrow-up-right" size={16} /><span class="visually-hidden"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a>
    </div>
  </section>

